From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Thu, 6 Oct 94 14:17:32 GMT
Subject: [#94-10-103] Mental Changes

 Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se> wrote: 
>
>Indeed. It would be wonderful if we could learn how to use such phenomena
>in a constructive way. Imagine being able to completely modify our
>personalities or belief to suit the situation. Unfortunately, so far they
>seem to be linked to intense mental or emotional stress in most cases,
>making them a bit unsafe ("Oops, the psychoprogrammer made a small error
>and now I'm a devout fundamentalist!"). Perhaps we should learn more about
>the mechanism and then modify it? 
>
This mechanism may *need* to be undetectable by the conscious levels
of the mind, since, by definition, it allows changes that you don't
want to be conscious of. What I'd like to change in that area is
having a more complete memory of the old state of mind, for purposes
of comparison, possible synthesis, and description of the change.
Do the extropians who became atheists suddenly remember what it
was like to be believers?

The only sudden mental change that I've been through happened when
an Alexander Technique teacher with a Buddhist background got
me to pay attention to someone else for a little longer than I
normally would. Usually, my mind would just skid off onto another
topic. (I now suspecti--for other reasons as well--that I've got ADD.)

Anyway, this caused a complete shift in my view of myself and of
fiction as well as my view of other people. As well as I can remember
it, I simply didn't have a way of thinking about people (including
myself) as coherent psychological entities--this is hard to talk
about because it's been some years, this sort of change
isn't part of the culture, and the change was so sudden and complete--
and therefore they weren't interesting to me. My best guess is
that I had missed a developmental stage and then went through
it very quickly.

I'd be interested in any questions people have about this--it might
help me clarify my thinking about what happened.

Nancy Lebovitz

