From: fhapgood@world.std.com (Fred Hapgood)
Date: Thu, 6 Oct 1994 11:20:17 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-105] Vinge talk

Yesterday Verner Vinge gave a talk at MIT re his thesis on the
Singularity.  This, as everybody here probably knows, is the
theory that trends in information processing hardware are going
to trigger the development of artifacts with a property he calls
"super-human intelligence".  Vinge never defined what he meant by
this, which is a problem, since at least to me the naked term
conveys no more than New Age phrases like higher planes of
consciousness and greater spiritual development.  He did throw
out an analogy, which was the evolution of H. sapiens.  The
development of "super-mammalian intelligence" had a lot of
consequences for their progenitors.  Suppose machines take a
similar step.  Maybe the consequences will be as unpredictable,
ungovernable, and uncomprehendable to us as cars and computers,
pollution and power blackouts, are to dogs and cats.

I just don't get it. Computers are tools.  A "super-intelligent"
tool is a just a tool that does its job, performs to
specifications, very well.  What is the problem with that? A
super-intelligent car would know where I wanted to go without
even asking.  A super-intelligent word processer would handle all
my correspondence automatically, showing me just the letters
that would amuse me.  A super-intelligent financial advisor would
start businesses on its own and mail me the dividend checks.  So
nu?

It is certainly possible that these super-intelligent tools
might start to do stuff on their own, stuff that had nothing to
do with our wishes.  Machines do that all time.  Behavior like
that is called being broken and when a machine expresses it we
fix it.

What's the issue?

