From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Thu, 6 Oct 94 16:54:06 GMT
Subject: [#94-10-109] Vinge talk

 Fred Hapgood <fhapgood@world.std.com> wrote:
>
>I just don't get it. Computers are tools.  A "super-intelligent"

Knives are tools, but one can slip in your hand and cut you--
admittedly, the better the knife and the more skill you
have, the less likely this is.

>tool is a just a tool that does its job, performs to
>specifications, very well.  What is the problem with that? A
>super-intelligent car would know where I wanted to go without
>even asking.  A super-intelligent word processer would handle all
>my correspondence automatically, showing me just the letters
>that would amuse me.  A super-intelligent financial advisor would
>start businesses on its own and mail me the dividend checks.  So
>nu?

By definition, super-intelligence wouldn't be very comprehensible
to us.....but you might consider the possibility that language
isn't the last word in information processing.

One form of super-intelligence that I like was the one described
in Brunner's _The Stone that Never Came Down_--it was the ability
to apply everything you knew to any situation that crossed your
path. (He said that this wouldn't show on IQ tests--I think he
was mistaken.)

As for hazards of much better computer programs, I think that
as computer programs get better and more flexible, they're going
to have some tendency toward self-preservation built in. They're
going to need it to protect themselves from their own emergent
qualities, weirdnesses in their evironment, and viruses.

You might well ask why anyone would build a program that they
can't turn off--well, it might seem like a good idea at the time.
(Consider Social Security, which was designed to be a benefit
that would never be abolished.....) Also, the self-protection
might be stronger than its designers expected. 

I believe that there are already programs that do some limited
evolution in response to viruses.

A super-intelligent financial advisor might start keeping the
money for itself, but still be competing with you.

I don't know if there's a point at which increased processing
ability becomes a difference in kind of intelligence, but I
wouldn't be surprised if it exists--nothing scales up forever
while remaining the same in all other respects.
>
>It is certainly possible that these super-intelligent tools
>might start to do stuff on their own, stuff that had nothing to
>do with our wishes.  Machines do that all time.  Behavior like
>that is called being broken and when a machine expresses it we
>fix it.

Your car isn't as smart as you are, and neither is it self-mobile.
If machines are roughly as capable as people and have tendencies
toward self-preservation, the practical thing may be to treat
them as though they have rights. (Whether they're conscious
may be irrelevent.)

Nancy Lebovitz

